What is a Package in Java?

A package is a folder that groups related Java classes and interfaces together.

In simple words:

A package is used to organize Java files into folders.
Real-Life Example 📁

Imagine your computer has this structure:

Documents
│
├── Photos
├── Videos
├── Songs
└── Projects

Why do we create these folders?

To keep files organized.
To find files easily.
To avoid confusion.

A Java package works the same

why use this
Why do we use packages?
1. Organize the project

All related classes are grouped together.

Example:

student/
    Student.java
    Result.java
    Attendance.java

2. Avoid class name conflicts

Suppose you have:

student/
    Student.java

employee/
    Student.java

Both classes are named Student.

Without packages, Java wouldn't know which one you mean.

With packages:
student.Student
employee.Student

Java can distinguish them.

3. Easier maintenance

Large companies may have thousands of Java files.

Packages help developers find files quickly.

4. Access control

Packages work together with access modifiers.

For example, default members can only be accessed within the same package.

How to create a package

Suppose your folder is:

Project/
└── student/
    └── Student.java

The first line of Student.java should be:

package student;
How to use a class from another package

Suppose:

Project/
│
├── Main.java
└── student/
    └── Student.java
Student.java
package student;

public class Student {

    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Hello Student");
    }
}
Main.java
import student.Student;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s = new Student();
        s.display();
    }
}
What does import do?
import student.Student;

This tells Java:

"Use the Student class from the student package."

Without the import, Java won't know where to find Student.

Real-world Example

Think of a college.

College
│
├── CSE Department
├── ECE Department
├── Mechanical Department
└── Civil Department
College = Project
Departments = Packages
Students = Classes

Each department has its own students, just like each package contains its own classes.

